WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:02.410
STEPHEN GREENBLATT: The area that we're standing

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:06.930
in London, the south bank of the Thames, was a kind of entertainment zone.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:09.390
You would come here from different parts of the city,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.390
knowing that there were lots of different things

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:14.700
you could do on a given afternoon.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.830
You could pay a boatman to take you across the river on his rowboat,

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:22.880
drop you off here.

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.190
And then you could go to The Globe, in front of which we're standing,

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:32.860
or across the street to another theater, The Swan.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.250
Or you could walk around in this area and go

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:40.640
to-- there were fields out beyond this zone where the theaters were.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.210
You could practice archery.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.799
You could go to one of the many houses of prostitution

00:00:45.799 --> 00:00:47.090
that were in this neighborhood.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.880
You could go to a tavern.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:53.580
You might go and see what were called bull baiting

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:55.630
and bear baiting exhibitions at the time.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:59.440
That is to say in spaces rather like this

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:08.870
where Londoners liked to watch a bull or a bear being attacked by savage dogs.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:13.450
They found this exciting or amusing.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:17.990
It was a practice that continued right up until the English Civil War

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.760
when Puritan soldiers killed most of the bears,

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.260
shot most of the bears of London.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.200
And they never quite recovered from that.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.690
But up until that time, all during Shakespeare's lifetime,

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.070
that was an alternative entertainment in this area.

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.050
There were lots of entertainments possible in the city

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.710
and particularly in this part of the city because it

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.440
was much less controlled by the city authorities

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.060
than the area on the other side of the river.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:53.030
This area had been the center of monasteries in the Middle Ages.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.580
And those monasteries had separate legal arrangements

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:01.920
from those that in the main part of the city businesses and houses had.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.710
And that meant that certain things could be done here

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:09.914
that the city fathers themselves didn't much like, but that they couldn't stop.

00:02:09.914 --> 00:02:11.830
Why did the city fathers not like the theater,

00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.280
which is one of the glories of London of the time?

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.000
Well, there were lots of reasons.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.570
They didn't like it because it was a traffic problem.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:23.410
There were lots of carriages that came, people riding up on horses.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.720
There was a legend in the seventh century

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.420
that Shakespeare got his start parking horses for people.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.180
So he was a kind of horse parking lot attendant that everyone loved.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:36.730
I don't believe it, but it's a story that

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.840
suggests that there were parking issues at the time.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.860
And then there were problems with people taking off from work.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.980
The city fathers, sober as they were, thought

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.180
that it was not a good idea to have a major entertainment industry that

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.900
depended on people abandoning their work and going off for the afternoon

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:55.780
to watch a play or to drink.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.000
Then they were worried about prostitution.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.800
They were worried about the possibility of people

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.550
saying things that were not somehow appropriate to be said,

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:08.870
that might be subversive or seditious.

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:11.170
And then they had the single biggest worry

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:16.880
that is I think something we need to take very, very seriously, which

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:18.520
was a public health issue.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:21.760
They worried, especially about bubonic plague.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.860
And they had good reason to worry about it.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:26.080
They were all through Shakespeare's lifetime,

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.940
and for that matter before even in the year that he was born,

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:33.310
outbreaks, terrible outbreaks of bubonic plague

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:38.270
that took away the lives of very large numbers of people,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:43.190
up to a third of the population of a city, of small towns and cities,

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:48.270
could die very, very quickly, from this extremely communicable disease.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.520
The city didn't understand exactly-- the medical authorities

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.480
didn't exist understand exactly what the disease came from.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.030
But they understood that it was intensified by large numbers of people

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.100
being brought together.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:06.200
So they thought, OK, we're not going to tell

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:11.940
people they can't go to church, because after all, God should protect people

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.530
from communicable diseases that way.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:16.490
But the theater, no.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:22.000
So when the death rate reached a certain number in London,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.330
the city fathers would shut the theaters down.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.430
And they wouldn't open them again until the death

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.320
rates declined below that number.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.560
And that happened actually periodically during Shakespeare's lifetime

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.830
and caused major problems, economic problems,

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.780
for companies like Shakespeare's and the other ones that were in competition.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:45.980
They would have to figure out what to do to wile away the weeks and sometimes

00:04:45.980 --> 00:04:51.320
months, and in one instance over a little more than a year,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.120
before the theaters could be reopened.

